	(text "From LED datasheet the Typical Voltage drop at 5 mA is 2.95V.\nKnowing this, I can compute the series resistor:\n	R = (3.3V - V_forw_LED) / 5 mA = 70 Ohm\n	--> E12 series (5% Toll.), R = 82 Ohm\n\nIf the Actual Forward Voltage of the LED is higher, it will simply light up with\nlower luminosity, so using the typical Forward Voltage value is ok (I don't need to \nbe precise on the actual current flowing in the LED, I just need it to light up)"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 52.832 241.808 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "0da14fe0-e786-4a7a-90c3-23363d84f856")
	)
	(text "Pull-Up is useful in normal operating conditions, because the\ncomparator output has Open-Drain output. The Pull-Down is used\nto set the ouput LOW when Power Supply 3V3 Analog is lost, so\nthat this Diagnostic pin returns a Fault indication to the reader (FPGA)\n\nPull-Down is 10 greater than Pull Up, so during normal operations,\nthe voltage for HIGH level is:\n	V_HIGH = 3.3V * 10R / (11R) = 3V\nThis voltage is more than enough (> V_IH = 1.7V) to be read as HIGH by the FPGA\n\nStatic consumption when output is LOW (condition with max consumption) is:\n	P_loss = (3.3^2) / 33k = 0.33 mW"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 297.688 211.074 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "2fe7876a-f573-4e97-9def-b223da0ef3b8")
	)
	(text "Connectors"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 353.822 226.822 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "34994a3a-fc66-4d7d-a67a-cfb20cfc687d")
	)
	(text "\"Fault Simulation Connector\", which just \ndisconnects the ADC Serial DataOut pin to\nthe reader. \nHiZ and GND faults can be simulated with\nthis connector "
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 377.698 45.72 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "3932f2db-6cc0-427e-b046-b75693cb78fa")
	)
	(text "FPGA Dev. Board"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 170.688 204.724 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "50843790-c014-463a-ae43-5359fb23a77c")
	)
	(text "HIGH	-> Sensor OK\nLOW		-> Sensor Fault"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 313.944 188.468 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "52ac14e9-3945-41dd-a603-55e8600c5679")
	)
	(text "HIGH	-> Sensor OK\nLOW		-> Sensor Fault"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 385.064 187.198 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "5601a247-7fb0-48af-ac8d-3e1f09656fa0")
	)
	(text "Power Supply"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 388.62 236.728 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
		)
		(uuid "624a5f53-46a2-4df0-99bf-3188e7ed8dd3")
	)
	(text "This Voltage Divider defines the Threshold under which\nthe VDIAG signal coming from the Sensor is interpreted\nas an error. VDIAG has two different operating states:\n	- NO ERROR:	VDIAG Voltage is always > 0.5V\n	- ERROR:	VDIAG Voltage is always < 0.39V\nIn practice, whatever threshold in the range [0.39V; 0.5V]\nis acceptable to recognise VDIAG errors. Because of this\nrange can not be achieved with both 5% Toll. resistors, \nand so one of them must be 1% (10.5k).\nThe \"Worst Case\" voltage values generated by the\nVoltage Divider are:\n\r\n	V_max 	= 3.366V * (10.605k / (10.605k + 64.6)) =\n			= 0.4747 V \n\n	V_min 	= 3.234V * (10.395k / (10.395k + 71.4k)) = \n			= 0.4110 V "
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 209.55 207.518 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "87c5bca4-30fa-48ef-83d1-f58fc3b4d321")
	)
	(text "Power Supply"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 69.596 194.31 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "8a1187db-295c-4430-8098-d9cce9d10554")
	)
	(text "\"Fault Simulation Connector\", which simulates a situation in\nwhich the Sensor is generating a VDIAG error (voltage under \n0.39 V).\n\nNo pull-down on the VDIAG signal, so the behaviour in the \ncase of Stuck-Open fault is not defined. This is done to not\nadd a resistor without purpose, because this connection is\ninside the PCB, and so it should not need protection."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 273.558 175.514 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "8ffeb4fc-27ce-4dde-a90d-f32ba582a66d")
	)
	(text "\"Fault Simulation Connector\", which disconnects power \nsupply for one of the two sensing channels.\nOnly the Stuck-Open fault can be simulated by this\nconnector"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 80.518 201.422 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "96de8feb-dbd0-46d1-9365-7bfd34c5dc19")
	)
	(text "Pull-Up resistors are used in the SPI CS inputs. The idea is that the default state of this control\npin (CS) in ensured even during FPGA's configuration phase (at startup time or after reset).\nCS = HIGH ensures that no ADC conversion is started\n\r\nPull-down resistors are used on the MUX selection inputs. The idea is to ensure a defined logic \nlevel even when the FPGA outputs are not yet configured (during startup or after reset), avoiding\npossible fast switching and undefined behaviors.\nSEL = LOW selects the S1 input, preventing the MUX from switching unpredictably due to \na floating control signal.\r\n\r\nThe resistor value is very high because this should not consume\nhigh static power:\n	P_loss = (3.3V)^2 / 100 kOhm = 0.11mW"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 237.236 237.49 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "a7171ddf-f7ce-4917-ba5c-dd4727eba0d9")
	)
	(text "Interlock"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 278.638 128.524 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "a90fa023-76bd-4b6f-9b4e-f15e7c37727f")
	)
	(text "Termination resistance for the MISO line. To be mounted as close as \npossible to the line driver (in theory near FPGA pin, but because of \ndevelopment board usage, place this resistor as near as possible to FPGA \nconnector).\n\nTo be mounted only if ringing problem arise on SPI (it is also possible \nto reduce the SPI frequency to mitigate the problem on the data lines, \nbut not on the CLK lines)"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 135.382 276.352 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "acb0e89c-2d30-4169-acbf-1c12e0090e82")
	)
	(text "Sensor"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 286.258 166.624 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "b9ccb04e-775b-43d5-8b44-15e592b7d311")
	)
	(text "This Voltage Divider must simulate the VDIAG signal \nwhen error is present (Voltage < 0.39V). Because \nprecision is not needed, 5% Toll. resistances are\nused, and the values are chosen guaranteeing the \ngenerated voltage is ALWAYS < 0.39V:\n\n	V_max 	= 3.3V * (10.5k / (10.5k + 95k)) = \n			= 0.335 V "
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 209.55 179.324 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "c9e1db25-ae94-4b2e-bb80-034a6be7cac8")
	)
	(text "ADC"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 363.474 14.986 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.286 2.286)
				(thickness 0.4572)
				(bold yes)
			)
		)
		(uuid "f4118976-0a5d-4edc-8ced-ed386807516c")
	)
	(text "The \"Sensor Board\" recives 5V Supply from its Master board, the \"Human Side Board\".\nThose 3.8V are DC/DC generated, and are fed through a cable harness, so they must \nconsidered noisy and not suitable for reference-sensitive Analog circuit. \n\nThis Power Supply circuit is developed using the WEBENCH TI Online Tool,\nallowing to define the LDO circuit that fits the design requirements. \nMore details in that report"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 17.78 203.2 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 0.762 0.762)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "fdd3813a-b4b6-4731-9539-5bcb2b89921f")
	)
